[' Here is a simple implementation of atoi() function in C, along with a main function that takes command line arguments and demonstrates its usage. This code does not use the standard library's atoi() function to keep things straightforward:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int my_atoi(const char *str) {
    int sign = 1, result = 0;

    while (*str == ' ') ++str;

    if (*str == '-') {
        sign = -1;
        ++str;
    } else if (*str == '+') ++str;

    while (*str >= '0' && *str <= '9') {
        result = result * 10 + (*(str++) - '0');
    }

    return sign * result;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s argument\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    int num = my_atoi(argv[1]);
    printf("Converted integer: %d\n", num);

    return 0;
}
```

With this code, you can compile and run it using a C compiler like gcc. An example of running the program would be `./a.out 42` which will output "Converted integer: 42". If you were to run it as `./a.out foo bar baz`, argc would be 4, and argv[1] would be "foo".']

[' Here's a simple C code that takes an index as an argument and prints the corresponding argument from the command-line arguments:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) { // Check if at least one argument is provided
        printf("Usage: ./argv_index index\n");
        return 1;
    }

    int index = atoi(argv[1]); // Convert the first command-line argument to an integer

    if (index < 1 || index > argc) { // Check if the provided index is valid
        printf("Index out of range\n");
        return 1;
    }

    int i = 0;
    for (i = 1; i < argc; ++i) {
        if (i == index) { // If the current iterator matches the given index, print it
            printf("%s\n", argv[i]);
            return 0;
        }
    }

    // In case no matching argument is found, provide an error message and exit with an error code
    printf("Argument not found\n");
    return 1;
}
```

Compile the code using gcc:

```bash
gcc -o argv_index argv_index.c
```

Now you can use it like this:

```bash
./argv_index 1 "Hello" "World" "from" "command line arguments"
// Outputs: World

./argv_index 3 "from" "command line arguments"
// Outputs: command line arguments
```']

