[' Here's a simple C code example using `popen()` and `printf()` functions to execute the `ls` command. Note that this code requires a Unix-like environment with a working shell (such as Bash, Sh, etc.).

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

void execute_command(const char *format, ...) {
    FILE *pipe;
    char buffer[128];
    va_list args;

    va_start(args, format);
    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);
    va_end(args);

    pipe = popen(buffer, "r");
    if (!pipe) {
        perror("popen");
        return;
    }

    while(!feof(pipe)) {
        if (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), pipe) != NULL)
            fputs(buffer, stdout);
    }

    pclose(pipe);
}

int main() {
    execute_command("ls -l\n");  // Pass the command as a string to the function
    return 0;
}
```

The `execute_command()` function takes a format string and variable number of arguments using the `va_list` mechanism. The given command is formatted with the required options (in this case, `ls -l`) and then passed to the function, which uses `popen()` to execute it and read its output.']

